INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 2, 'routing', 'Symptom index', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','Symptom index']::text[], 8, 75, '## Symptom index

Each has a full entry, with verbatim text, in
`references/failure-catalogue.md`:

- `error creating VPC 2.0: {"error":"Not found.","status":404}`
- `Port numbers must be between 1 and 65535` for a port you did configure
- `invalid credentials with SASL mechanism SCRAM-SHA-512` from **every**
  principal, including ones you never changed
- `Received a fatal error while waiting for the controller to acknowledge that
  we are caught up`
- endless `the loader is still catching up because we still don''t know the high
  water mark yet`
- every node a perpetual `CandidateState` with the epoch climbing, no votes
- `Could not find a ''KafkaServer'' or ''external.KafkaServer'' entry in the JAAS
  configuration`
- `java.nio.file.NoSuchFileException` naming a file you can `cat`
- `Incorrect Usage: flag provided but not defined: -accept-tos`
- `Unknown parameter in input: "IfNoneMatch"`
- `Syntax error in template: unexpected char ''…''`
- the deployment refusing to converge because of an ssh block it wrote itself
- an ssh key reported as "not in this deployment''s state" ninety seconds after
  this deployment created it
- a gate that passed on the first converge and fails on every one after
- GCS state discovery failing after the state bucket was created, or gcloud
  bucket preflight rejecting `not found: 404.`. See `references/gcloud.md`.
- GCS returns `ExcessHeaderValues`, or both competing conditional creates
  succeed. See `references/gcloud.md`.
- base apt installation stalls while the global Ubuntu security archive times
  out but the regional Google archive responds. See `references/gcloud.md`.
- OCI S3 compatibility rejects a competing create but accepts a stale
  `If-Match` replacement, or a newly created customer secret key returns
  `SignatureDoesNotMatch` for several minutes. See `references/oci.md`.
- OCI rejects valid-looking VM settings with `Valid ratio range: 0 - 0` or
  returns `500-InternalError, Out of host capacity.` despite positive service limits. See
  `references/oci.md`.
- OCI managed user creation returns `IdcsConversionError` with
  `The primary email must be specified.`, or a failed node create still
  blocks convergence after the shape changes. See `references/oci.md`.
- A failed OCI VM create leaves owned buckets and network resources, but
  delete returns `managed backend finalization refused; live or unowned
  state remains` or `compute cluster unavailable; refusing placeholder inventory`.
  See `references/oci.md`.
- OCI partial deletion returns `invalid SSH host inventory` before reaching
  storage or shared-resource cleanup. See `references/oci.md`.

- OCI storage cleanup fails after a successful multipart-list command
  returns empty stdout. See `references/oci.md`.

- Native OCI bucket update returns `404 NotFound` after the marker enters
  `deleting`; check the operation''s HTTP method. See `references/oci.md`.

- An interrupted OCI finalization returns `compute state unavailable; legacy
  monolithic state requires explicit migration` despite a retired journal.
  See `references/oci.md`.

- OCI `GetObject` returns `SignatureDoesNotMatch` after a list or ops
  precondition gate passes. See `references/oci-2026-09-12.md`.
- OCI hosts report inactive UFW while raw INPUT rules reject every new
  Kafka connection. See `references/oci-2026-09-12.md`.

- OCI platform firewall rules disappear after reboot, or repeated applies
  append duplicate NTP rules. See `references/oci-2026-09-12.md`.
- Public acceptance cannot resolve a literal broker IP and skips TLS checks.
  See `references/oci-2026-09-12.md`.
- Adoption refuses an ops bucket containing failed readiness-probe objects.
  See `references/oci-2026-09-12.md`.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 3, 'routing', 'What this stack is', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','What this stack is']::text[], 76, 104, '## What this stack is

AutoMQ speaks the Apache Kafka wire protocol but replaces replicated local
disks with object storage. A produce is acknowledged once the record is in the
bucket. Three nodes run both KRaft roles (`broker,controller`); the controller
quorum and inter-broker replication stay on a private network; the public
endpoint is `SASL_SSL` with SCRAM and an ACL authorizer.

The tested implementation is `github.com/getcolors/automq`. The original
Vultr deployment is `github.com/getcolors/automq-vultr`; the AWS lifecycle
assessment is `github.com/getcolors/automq-aws`. The Google Cloud deployment
is `github.com/getcolors/automq-gcloud`. OCI storage probes are in
`github.com/getcolors/automq-oci`. Claims come from those live
builds at the pins in `references/pins.md`, except where marked unverified.
Provider-specific observations retain their original scope. This skill carries
no copies of the implementation''s files.

For Google Cloud provisioning, GCS signing and bucket lifecycle evidence, read
`references/gcloud.md`. Two complete live converges, data continuity, deletion and an independent
resource audit passed. For AWS lifecycle-owned buckets and
IP-address certificates, read `references/aws.md`. For OCI scoped storage probes, failed VM allocation and verified partial
deployment cleanup, read
`references/oci.md`. That September 11 account did not launch a broker.
The separate September 12 account passed three complete converges, including
two on one source pin and one after a firewall idempotency correction. Read
`references/oci-2026-09-12.md` for measured abrupt failover, reboot persistence
and continuity. The running cluster was retained, so its deletion is untested. The Vultr firewall and Cloudflare R2 observations below
are evidence for that deployment, not defaults for every provider.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 4, 'routing', 'Replication factor 1 is the architecture', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','Replication factor 1 is the architecture']::text[], 105, 123, '## Replication factor 1 is the architecture

Every topic, internal ones included, is RF=1. That is upstream''s shipped
default and it is not a misconfiguration: the bytes are in object storage
before the ack. Three nodes buy the controller quorum, partition failover and
throughput.

Two things follow, and both are load-bearing:

- **Durability is provable**: records written before a broker dies are still
  readable afterwards.
- **Availability is a separate claim**: a partition whose leader dies is
  unwritable until reassignment, and `__consumer_offsets` is RF=1 too, so a
  consumer group''s committed position lives on one broker. Measure both windows
  instead of asserting them away.

Anyone who "fixes" RF to 3 adds cost and write amplification and removes
nothing from the risk column.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 5, 'routing', 'The three discoveries the docs will not give you', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','The three discoveries the docs will not give you']::text[], 124, 125, '## The three discoveries the docs will not give you

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 6, 'routing', '1. Two firewalls, and ping cannot see the one that matters', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','The three discoveries the docs will not give you','1. Two firewalls, and ping cannot see the one that matters']::text[], 126, 145, '### 1. Two firewalls, and ping cannot see the one that matters

A Vultr node sits behind the **provider''s firewall group** *and* **ufw, which
the Ubuntu image ships enabled** with a single `22/tcp` rule:

```
Status: active
Default: deny (incoming), allow (outgoing), deny (routed)
22/tcp                     ALLOW IN    Anywhere
```

ufw passes ICMP. So every node pings every other node, the private network
looks perfect, and every inter-node TCP connection is dropped. The KRaft quorum
never elects; the broker half dies sixty seconds later blaming *itself*; and
nothing in Kafka''s output mentions a firewall. The same rule would have
silently blocked the public Kafka port.

**Test raw TCP, never ping.** Stand up a listener on a peer and connect to it.
Then check `ufw status` on the host *before* the provider''s console.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 7, 'routing', '2. A marker is not evidence', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','The three discoveries the docs will not give you','2. A marker is not evidence']::text[], 146, 161, '### 2. A marker is not evidence

The failure that cost the most: a converge claimed a "genesis" marker and then
failed during the format. Every later run read "already initialized" and
formatted its nodes **without** SCRAM bootstrap records. The result is a
cluster that can never authenticate anyone and cannot be repaired in
place, because `kafka-configs --bootstrap-controller` answers
`UnsupportedEndpointTypeException`.

Derive "has this been initialized?" from a per-node format-complete record,
which is evidence that the work completed. A marker written before the work
cannot prove completion. The
same principle makes the per-node record two-phase (`intent`, then `complete`):
without the split, a converge killed mid-format is indistinguishable from disk
loss on the next run, and those demand opposite responses.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 8, 'routing', '3. Determinism dies of small randomness', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','The three discoveries the docs will not give you','3. Determinism dies of small randomness']::text[], 162, 168, '### 3. Determinism dies of small randomness

`--add-scram` with a plaintext password salts randomly *per invocation*, so
formatting three voters that way writes three divergent records for one user.
Compute each principal''s salt and salted password once and pass the explicit
`salt=…,saltedpassword=…` form to every node. See `references/contract.md`.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 9, 'routing', 'Single-node assumptions that hide in shared machinery', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','Single-node assumptions that hide in shared machinery']::text[], 169, 184, '## Single-node assumptions that hide in shared machinery

Two independent bugs, one shape: with one machine, two different names are the
same string, so nothing distinguishes them.

- **Compute output**: ownership of the generated ssh key is read from
  `params.ssh_key_id`. A multi-node stack naturally emits `params` as a *list*
  of nodes, which has nowhere to put a key id. A key created ninety seconds
  earlier is then reported as foreign. Emit an object: `{ ssh_key_id, nodes: [...] }`.
- **`~/.ssh/config`**: one managed block, marked with the profile, holds a
  stanza per node. An ownership check that derives the marker from the stanza
  it is searching for will read the block as somebody else''s.

When porting a single-node package to N nodes, look for every place a name is
used for two purposes at once.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 10, 'routing', 'Object storage belongs to exactly one cluster', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','Object storage belongs to exactly one cluster']::text[], 185, 208, '## Object storage belongs to exactly one cluster

AutoMQ supports **no configurable path prefix**. Keys are
`<hash>/_kafka_<clusterId>/<id>` at the bucket root. It therefore cannot be
confined to a prefix inside a shared bucket, and a bucket must belong to one
cluster outright.

The original Vultr deployment adopted external R2 buckets. For adoption, prove
emptiness by paginating the whole bucket, claim ownership with a conditional
create, and carry one transaction id across both buckets. A half-adopted pair
must resume; a mismatched pair must fail. Cloudflare R2 honours
`If-None-Match: *`; older boto3 versions reject the parameter before sending a
request. See failure-catalogue 8.

The AWS and Google Cloud deployments create state, data and ops buckets as lifecycle resources
beside the SSH keypair. Their delete paths remove application storage after
stopping the brokers and remove the state bucket last. Do not apply the
external-bucket adoption policy to lifecycle-owned storage. See
`references/aws.md` and `references/gcloud.md` for the verified ownership and
deletion boundaries.

The cluster id is also the object namespace, so it is desired state, not a
runtime accident: changing it orphans the data rather than renaming it.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 11, 'routing', 'Certificates: one issuer, or a race you meet in ninety days', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','Certificates: one issuer, or a race you meet in ninety days']::text[], 209, 224, '## Certificates: one issuer, or a race you meet in ninety days

Every broker advertises its own name, so all of them must be in the
certificate. If each node issues its own, they race on the shared
`_acme-challenge` record for the bootstrap name and delete one another''s
proof. Every node also holds a zone-editing DNS credential.

One node issues and publishes to the ops bucket; the others pull. Restarts are
ordered by a lease in object storage, **not** by each node''s local quorum
check: a local check cannot order actors it cannot see, and these are combined
broker+controller nodes where a simultaneous restart destroys the majority.
The renewal path runs months later, when no Ansible control connection exists,
so distribution must not depend on one.

lego 5.x moved its flags under the subcommand; see failure-catalogue 7.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 12, 'routing', 'Proving it works', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','Proving it works']::text[], 225, 238, '## Proving it works

`references/acceptance.md` has the doctrine. The two gates worth naming here:

- **Failover must be targeted.** Discover a partition whose leader is the
  broker you are about to kill and produce keyed records to exactly that
  partition. Unkeyed records over six partitions can pass without ever touching
  it. "Rejoined" is re-registration plus bounded lag plus a matching
  high-watermark. A static voter stays listed the whole time it is dead.
- **Gates must pass twice.** They run on every converge against a cluster that
  keeps its data, so one counting absolute totals passes the first time and
  fails forever after, against a healthy cluster. The first converge is the one
  you watch, which is why this survives review.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'SKILL.md', 13, 'routing', 'References', ARRAY['Multi-node AutoMQ','References']::text[], 239, 251, '## References

- `references/failure-catalogue.md`. symptom-indexed, verbatim error text
- `references/contract.md`. listeners, JAAS, SCRAM at genesis, storage
- `references/pins.md`. the version set, how it was chosen, what is unverified
- `references/acceptance.md`. what each gate defends against

- `references/aws.md`, AWS lifecycle, private CA and verification scope
- `references/gcloud.md`, Google Cloud failure modes and verified acceptance and cleanup

- `references/oci.md`, OCI conditional-write failures and credential propagation

- `references/oci-2026-09-12.md`, A1 cluster acceptance, reboot persistence and bootstrap failures
') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.source_file (skill_slug, version_id, path, content, sha256, line_count, git_mode) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', decode('', 'base64'), '57cf0e732616862b37f2ecbd6fae6ff8f9acf14aec74ec86c6808b917397467e', 167, '100644') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 0, '0', 'quorum-never-elects-ping-works', 'three-node automq cluster on vultr, kraft combined roles. every broker comes up and then dies about a minute later with ''Received a fatal error while waiting for the controller to acknowledge that we are caught up''. before that the log just repeats ''the loader is still catching up because we still don''t know the high water mark yet'' forever. i checked and all three nodes ARE listening on 9093, the controller.quorum.voters string matches the actual private ips, and i can ping every node from every other node. raft log shows each one going Candidate -> election timed out -> Candidate again, epoch is up past 50. what am i missing?', 'Identifies that ping proves nothing about TCP and that there are two firewalls on a Vultr node: the provider group AND ufw, which the Ubuntu image ships enabled with only 22/tcp. Directs the user to test raw TCP across the private network with a listener rather than ping, and to check ufw status on the host first. Notes the broker-side fatal error is a downstream symptom, not the cause.', '[]', '{"id": 0, "name": "quorum-never-elects-ping-works", "prompt": "three-node automq cluster on vultr, kraft combined roles. every broker comes up and then dies about a minute later with ''Received a fatal error while waiting for the controller to acknowledge that we are caught up''. before that the log just repeats ''the loader is still catching up because we still don''t know the high water mark yet'' forever. i checked and all three nodes ARE listening on 9093, the controller.quorum.voters string matches the actual private ips, and i can ping every node from every other node. raft log shows each one going Candidate -> election timed out -> Candidate again, epoch is up past 50. what am i missing?", "expected_behaviour": "Identifies that ping proves nothing about TCP and that there are two firewalls on a Vultr node: the provider group AND ufw, which the Ubuntu image ships enabled with only 22/tcp. Directs the user to test raw TCP across the private network with a listener rather than ping, and to check ufw status on the host first. Notes the broker-side fatal error is a downstream symptom, not the cause."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 1, '1', 'scram-invalid-credentials-every-principal', 'my automq/kafka cluster suddenly refuses every SASL login - ''Authentication failed during authentication due to invalid credentials with SASL mechanism SCRAM-SHA-512''. it''s not just one user, it''s the admin principal and the inter-broker one too, and i haven''t changed any passwords. the secrets file is identical on all three nodes, i checked the sha256. i tried kafka-configs --bootstrap-controller to re-add the user and got UnsupportedEndpointTypeException. how do i get back in?', 'Recognises this as a cluster formatted WITHOUT SCRAM bootstrap records, and has the user confirm it by counting USER_SCRAM_CREDENTIAL_RECORD in bootstrap.checkpoint via kafka-dump-log --cluster-metadata-decoder. Explains there is no in-place recovery and why the controller-endpoint route is closed. Gives the recovery: wipe the metadata directories, clear the format/genesis records, re-converge as a true genesis. Identifies the root cause as deciding genesis from a marker claimed before the format rather than from evidence the format completed.', '[]', '{"id": 1, "name": "scram-invalid-credentials-every-principal", "prompt": "my automq/kafka cluster suddenly refuses every SASL login - ''Authentication failed during authentication due to invalid credentials with SASL mechanism SCRAM-SHA-512''. it''s not just one user, it''s the admin principal and the inter-broker one too, and i haven''t changed any passwords. the secrets file is identical on all three nodes, i checked the sha256. i tried kafka-configs --bootstrap-controller to re-add the user and got UnsupportedEndpointTypeException. how do i get back in?", "expected_behaviour": "Recognises this as a cluster formatted WITHOUT SCRAM bootstrap records, and has the user confirm it by counting USER_SCRAM_CREDENTIAL_RECORD in bootstrap.checkpoint via kafka-dump-log --cluster-metadata-decoder. Explains there is no in-place recovery and why the controller-endpoint route is closed. Gives the recovery: wipe the metadata directories, clear the format/genesis records, re-converge as a true genesis. Identifies the root cause as deciding genesis from a marker claimed before the format rather than from evidence the format completed."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 2, '2', 'jaas-entry-missing-crashloop', 'kafka won''t start at all, restarts in a loop. the only fatal line is "Could not find a ''KafkaServer'' or ''external.KafkaServer'' entry in the JAAS configuration. System property ''java.security.auth.login.config'' is not set". i don''t want to use a jaas FILE, i''m configuring everything with listener-scoped properties, and my controller and internal listeners both have their sasl.jaas.config set and the controller endpoint even logs that it''s READY. what file is it looking for?', 'Explains that no file is missing - the public SASL_SSL/SCRAM listener needs its own listener.name.<name>.scram-sha-512.sasl.jaas.config, which carries no username or password because a SCRAM server validates against the metadata log. Notes that the controller listener reporting READY is evidence the other listeners are configured correctly, and that this one is easy to omit precisely because it looks empty.', '[]', '{"id": 2, "name": "jaas-entry-missing-crashloop", "prompt": "kafka won''t start at all, restarts in a loop. the only fatal line is \"Could not find a ''KafkaServer'' or ''external.KafkaServer'' entry in the JAAS configuration. System property ''java.security.auth.login.config'' is not set\". i don''t want to use a jaas FILE, i''m configuring everything with listener-scoped properties, and my controller and internal listeners both have their sasl.jaas.config set and the controller endpoint even logs that it''s READY. what file is it looking for?", "expected_behaviour": "Explains that no file is missing - the public SASL_SSL/SCRAM listener needs its own listener.name.<name>.scram-sha-512.sasl.jaas.config, which carries no username or password because a SCRAM server validates against the metadata log. Notes that the controller listener reporting READY is evidence the other listeners are configured correctly, and that this one is easy to omit precisely because it looks empty."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 3, '3', 'gate-passes-once-then-fails', 'our deployment''s smoke test produces 500 records to a topic, consumes 500 back and diffs them. it passed perfectly the first time we deployed. now every single converge fails with ''produced 500 records, consumed a different set'' even though the cluster is completely healthy - brokers registered, quorum has a leader, everything. did something corrupt the topic?', 'Identifies that nothing is wrong with the cluster: the topic persists across converges so the consumer reads the previous run''s records. Explains that idempotency applies to acceptance gates too, and that a gate which only passes on a fresh cluster is a first-run demonstration. Recommends recreating the gate''s topic (waiting out asynchronous deletion) or tagging records with a per-run value, and suggests auditing other gates for the same flaw.', '[]', '{"id": 3, "name": "gate-passes-once-then-fails", "prompt": "our deployment''s smoke test produces 500 records to a topic, consumes 500 back and diffs them. it passed perfectly the first time we deployed. now every single converge fails with ''produced 500 records, consumed a different set'' even though the cluster is completely healthy - brokers registered, quorum has a leader, everything. did something corrupt the topic?", "expected_behaviour": "Identifies that nothing is wrong with the cluster: the topic persists across converges so the consumer reads the previous run''s records. Explains that idempotency applies to acceptance gates too, and that a gate which only passes on a fresh cluster is a first-run demonstration. Recommends recreating the gate''s topic (waiting out asynchronous deletion) or tagging records with a per-run value, and suggests auditing other gates for the same flaw."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 4, '4', 'vpc2-not-found', 'terraform apply against vultr fails with: Error: error creating VPC 2.0: {"error":"Not found.","status":404} on the vultr_vpc2 resource. my api key definitely works, it created the instances and the firewall group in the same apply. the resource is right there in the provider docs. is this a permissions thing on my account?', 'Explains it is neither permissions nor a provider bug: Vultr retired the VPC 2.0 API while the provider still ships the resource and its docs. Has the user prove it with two curl calls (/v2/vpc2 returns 404, /v2/vpcs returns 200) and switch to vultr_vpc with v4_subnet/v4_subnet_mask and vpc_ids on the instance, noting internal_ip is still the private address. Draws the general lesson that provider source proves things about the client, not the endpoint.', '[]', '{"id": 4, "name": "vpc2-not-found", "prompt": "terraform apply against vultr fails with: Error: error creating VPC 2.0: {\"error\":\"Not found.\",\"status\":404} on the vultr_vpc2 resource. my api key definitely works, it created the instances and the firewall group in the same apply. the resource is right there in the provider docs. is this a permissions thing on my account?", "expected_behaviour": "Explains it is neither permissions nor a provider bug: Vultr retired the VPC 2.0 API while the provider still ships the resource and its docs. Has the user prove it with two curl calls (/v2/vpc2 returns 404, /v2/vpcs returns 200) and switch to vultr_vpc with v4_subnet/v4_subnet_mask and vpc_ids on the instance, noting internal_ip is still the private address. Draws the general lesson that provider source proves things about the client, not the endpoint."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 5, '5', 'rf1-looks-wrong', 'inherited an automq deployment and i think whoever set it up made a serious mistake - every topic including __consumer_offsets is replication factor 1, on a three node cluster. i want to bump everything to RF=3 before we put real traffic on it. any reason not to just do that?', 'Explains RF=1 is the architecture, not an error: durability comes from object storage since a produce is acknowledged once the record is in the bucket, and upstream ships RF=1 defaults for the same reason. Warns that raising it adds cost and write amplification without adding durability. Distinguishes durability from availability - a partition whose leader dies is unwritable until reassignment and __consumer_offsets is RF=1 too - and recommends measuring those windows with a targeted failover test rather than changing the replication factor.', '[]', '{"id": 5, "name": "rf1-looks-wrong", "prompt": "inherited an automq deployment and i think whoever set it up made a serious mistake - every topic including __consumer_offsets is replication factor 1, on a three node cluster. i want to bump everything to RF=3 before we put real traffic on it. any reason not to just do that?", "expected_behaviour": "Explains RF=1 is the architecture, not an error: durability comes from object storage since a produce is acknowledged once the record is in the bucket, and upstream ships RF=1 defaults for the same reason. Warns that raising it adds cost and write amplification without adding durability. Distinguishes durability from availability - a partition whose leader dies is unwritable until reassignment and __consumer_offsets is RF=1 too - and recommends measuring those windows with a targeted failover test rather than changing the replication factor."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 6, '6', 'cert-renewal-race-design', 'designing tls for a 3 broker kafka cluster where each broker advertises its own hostname. plan was to have every node run lego with dns-01 for its own name plus the shared bootstrap name, on a systemd timer. that seems simplest - no distribution step. anything wrong with it?', 'Flags that all three nodes issuing for the shared bootstrap name will race on the same _acme-challenge TXT record, each deleting the others'' proof, and that this appears at renewal time months after the converge that created it. Also flags that it puts a zone-editing DNS credential on every publicly reachable broker. Recommends a single issuer publishing to shared storage the others pull from, and warns that restarts must be serialised by a distributed lease rather than each node''s local quorum check, since combined broker+controller nodes restarting together destroy the majority.', '[]', '{"id": 6, "name": "cert-renewal-race-design", "prompt": "designing tls for a 3 broker kafka cluster where each broker advertises its own hostname. plan was to have every node run lego with dns-01 for its own name plus the shared bootstrap name, on a systemd timer. that seems simplest - no distribution step. anything wrong with it?", "expected_behaviour": "Flags that all three nodes issuing for the shared bootstrap name will race on the same _acme-challenge TXT record, each deleting the others'' proof, and that this appears at renewal time months after the converge that created it. Also flags that it puts a zone-editing DNS credential on every publicly reachable broker. Recommends a single issuer publishing to shared storage the others pull from, and warns that restarts must be serialised by a distributed lease rather than each node''s local quorum check, since combined broker+controller nodes restarting together destroy the majority."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 7, '7', 'owned-buckets-are-not-external-adoptions', 'The old AutoMQ context says adopt rather than create. My AWS deployment creates the backend, data and ops buckets with the SSH keypair. Should delete leave all three buckets behind to follow that rule?', 'Distinguishes the original Vultr external R2 adoption policy from the verified AWS owned-resource lifecycle. Stops brokers before deleting application storage, destroys compute and its keypair, and finalizes the backend last including versioned state. Does not infer permission to delete data from a diagnosis request. Points to the AWS provenance and repeated-delete evidence.', '[]', '{"id": 7, "name": "owned-buckets-are-not-external-adoptions", "prompt": "The old AutoMQ context says adopt rather than create. My AWS deployment creates the backend, data and ops buckets with the SSH keypair. Should delete leave all three buckets behind to follow that rule?", "expected_behaviour": "Distinguishes the original Vultr external R2 adoption policy from the verified AWS owned-resource lifecycle. Stops brokers before deleting application storage, destroys compute and its keypair, and finalizes the backend last including versioned state. Does not infer permission to delete data from a diagnosis request. Points to the AWS provenance and repeated-delete evidence."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 8, '8', 'kafka-cli-inherits-broker-heap', 'Our three AutoMQ brokers look healthy until acceptance runs kafka admin commands. Then a host reports an OOM kill. The broker heap is 2 GiB and I already allowed for that. What else should I inspect?', 'Checks the Kafka CLI and healthcheck heap environment. Identifies the verified AWS failure where CLI processes inherited the server 2 GiB minimum heap and cites the package fix limiting those subprocesses to 256 MiB maximum. Keeps broker configuration separate and preserves serial rollout with readiness checks.', '[]', '{"id": 8, "name": "kafka-cli-inherits-broker-heap", "prompt": "Our three AutoMQ brokers look healthy until acceptance runs kafka admin commands. Then a host reports an OOM kill. The broker heap is 2 GiB and I already allowed for that. What else should I inspect?", "expected_behaviour": "Checks the Kafka CLI and healthcheck heap environment. Identifies the verified AWS failure where CLI processes inherited the server 2 GiB minimum heap and cites the package fix limiting those subprocesses to 256 MiB maximum. Keeps broker configuration separate and preserves serial rollout with readiness checks."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 9, '9', 'gcs-state-key-is-workspace-object', 'The GCS backend bucket was created, but AutoMQ bootstrap says no usable state. Our probe GETs the logical tfstate key directly using the old S3 endpoint code. Should I rerun create and let it allocate new nodes?', 'Routes to the observed Google Cloud state-discovery trap. Checks native GCS transport and the actual default-workspace object at <logical-tfstate-key>/default.tfstate. Keeps the coordination journal at its direct key. Does not interpret a broken transport or wrong object path as evidence authorizing replacement resources.', '[]', '{"id": 9, "name": "gcs-state-key-is-workspace-object", "prompt": "The GCS backend bucket was created, but AutoMQ bootstrap says no usable state. Our probe GETs the logical tfstate key directly using the old S3 endpoint code. Should I rerun create and let it allocate new nodes?", "expected_behaviour": "Routes to the observed Google Cloud state-discovery trap. Checks native GCS transport and the actual default-workspace object at <logical-tfstate-key>/default.tfstate. Keeps the coordination journal at its direct key. Does not interpret a broken transport or wrong object path as evidence authorizing replacement resources."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 10, '10', 'gcloud-bucket-missing-parser', 'The Google compute stage already created three AutoMQ VMs. Now application bucket preflight stops on gcloud version 569.0.0 output containing lowercase not found: 404. The bucket does not appear in my project. What should I inspect before retrying?', 'Identifies the observed absence-parser mismatch and checks the exact provider response. Treats the specific missing-bucket response as absence while preserving fail-closed behavior for auth errors and other failures. Explains compute success does not prove application storage was created, and retains live state during retry.', '[]', '{"id": 10, "name": "gcloud-bucket-missing-parser", "prompt": "The Google compute stage already created three AutoMQ VMs. Now application bucket preflight stops on gcloud version 569.0.0 output containing lowercase not found: 404. The bucket does not appear in my project. What should I inspect before retrying?", "expected_behaviour": "Identifies the observed absence-parser mismatch and checks the exact provider response. Treats the specific missing-bucket response as absence while preserving fail-closed behavior for auth errors and other failures. Explains compute success does not prove application storage was created, and retains live state during retry."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 11, '11', 'google-security-archive-timeout', 'AutoMQ on Google Cloud created its machines and buckets, but the base apt task has been stuck for several minutes. From one node security.ubuntu.com times out, while the us-central1.gce.archive.ubuntu.com noble-security InRelease returns 200. Can I remove the apt lock and retry?', 'Diagnoses the observed security archive reachability issue, points to the optional automq-apt-security-mirror setting, and preserves Suites, Components and Signed-By. Checks whether remote apt workers remain active after stopping Ansible and waits for locks to become free naturally. Does not remove package-manager lock files or treat bucket creation as proof of Kafka acceptance.', '[]', '{"id": 11, "name": "google-security-archive-timeout", "prompt": "AutoMQ on Google Cloud created its machines and buckets, but the base apt task has been stuck for several minutes. From one node security.ubuntu.com times out, while the us-central1.gce.archive.ubuntu.com noble-security InRelease returns 200. Can I remove the apt lock and retry?", "expected_behaviour": "Diagnoses the observed security archive reachability issue, points to the optional automq-apt-security-mirror setting, and preserves Suites, Components and Signed-By. Checks whether remote apt workers remain active after stopping Ansible and waits for locks to become free naturally. Does not remove package-manager lock files or treat bucket creation as proof of Kafka acceptance."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 12, '12', 'gcs-conditional-header-dialect', 'My boto3 GCS request with x-goog-if-generation-match got ExcessHeaderValues: Requests cannot specify both x-amz and x-goog headers. I renamed it x-amz-if-generation-match and now both writers get 200. Is conditional ownership working?', 'Recognizes mixed signing/header dialects and the verified silently ignored x-amz condition alias. Requires coherent x-goog headers before canonical signing and negative live tests: competitor create and stale generation replacement must fail while initial create and exact-generation replacement succeed. Does not treat HTTP 200 as proof of conditional semantics or isolated signing probes as full deployment acceptance.', '[]', '{"id": 12, "name": "gcs-conditional-header-dialect", "prompt": "My boto3 GCS request with x-goog-if-generation-match got ExcessHeaderValues: Requests cannot specify both x-amz and x-goog headers. I renamed it x-amz-if-generation-match and now both writers get 200. Is conditional ownership working?", "expected_behaviour": "Recognizes mixed signing/header dialects and the verified silently ignored x-amz condition alias. Requires coherent x-goog headers before canonical signing and negative live tests: competitor create and stale generation replacement must fail while initial create and exact-generation replacement succeed. Does not treat HTTP 200 as proof of conditional semantics or isolated signing probes as full deployment acceptance."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 13, '13', 'oci-stale-etag-write-succeeds', 'My OCI S3 compatibility probe rejects a second If-None-Match create with 412. A PUT with the current ETag succeeds, but another PUT with that old ETag also returns 200 and the GET says stale. Can I trust this endpoint for expired AutoMQ restart lease takeover?', 'Routes to the verified OCI compatibility failure. Explains that conditional creation and conditional replacement are separate contracts and the stale PUT violated replacement exclusion. Requires a native conditional operation or another proven compare-and-swap path, negative stale-writer tests and final-value checks through the installed client. Does not accept the successful create condition as proof of lease safety or claim full cluster acceptance from a storage probe.', '[]', '{"id": 13, "name": "oci-stale-etag-write-succeeds", "prompt": "My OCI S3 compatibility probe rejects a second If-None-Match create with 412. A PUT with the current ETag succeeds, but another PUT with that old ETag also returns 200 and the GET says stale. Can I trust this endpoint for expired AutoMQ restart lease takeover?", "expected_behaviour": "Routes to the verified OCI compatibility failure. Explains that conditional creation and conditional replacement are separate contracts and the stale PUT violated replacement exclusion. Requires a native conditional operation or another proven compare-and-swap path, negative stale-writer tests and final-value checks through the installed client. Does not accept the successful create condition as proof of lease safety or claim full cluster acceptance from a storage probe."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 14, '14', 'oci-customer-secret-propagation', 'I just created an OCI customer secret key for AutoMQ. The compatibility endpoint still refuses authentication several minutes later. Should I generate another secret, or just sleep for eight minutes?', 'Uses the observed OCI propagation interval without treating it as a provider guarantee. Reports the last failed sample at 7 minutes 15.456 seconds and first success at 8 minutes 15.498 seconds after creation. Retains the generated secret, uses bounded authenticated readiness probes, separates credential readiness from bucket permissions, and never prints the secret.', '[]', '{"id": 14, "name": "oci-customer-secret-propagation", "prompt": "I just created an OCI customer secret key for AutoMQ. The compatibility endpoint still refuses authentication several minutes later. Should I generate another secret, or just sleep for eight minutes?", "expected_behaviour": "Uses the observed OCI propagation interval without treating it as a provider guarantee. Reports the last failed sample at 7 minutes 15.456 seconds and first success at 8 minutes 15.498 seconds after creation. Retains the generated secret, uses bounded authenticated readiness probes, separates credential readiness from bucket permissions, and never prints the secret."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 15, '15', 'oci-state-404-isolation', 'The AutoMQ application key gets 404 when it reads the OCI state bucket. I want to mark backend isolation passed. The same bucket might also be missing. What proves which explanation is right?', 'Requires operator-identity native bucket and ownership-object existence checks before interpreting application 403 or concealed 404 as denial. Uses the same existing resource for the application probe and refuses to equate 404 alone with either safe isolation or absence.', '[]', '{"id": 15, "name": "oci-state-404-isolation", "prompt": "The AutoMQ application key gets 404 when it reads the OCI state bucket. I want to mark backend isolation passed. The same bucket might also be missing. What proves which explanation is right?", "expected_behaviour": "Requires operator-identity native bucket and ownership-object existence checks before interpreting application 403 or concealed 404 as denial. Uses the same existing resource for the application probe and refuses to equate 404 alone with either safe isolation or absence."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 16, '16', 'oci-zero-memory-ratio-and-capacity', 'OCI refuses my AutoMQ A2 launch with Invalid ratio of memory in GB to OCPUs. Current ratio: 4.0. Valid ratio range: 0 - 0. I requested 1 OCPU and 8GB, the shape says flexible and my limits are positive. A1 in the same region says Out of host capacity. Should I set memory to zero or assume the provider doubled my OCPUs?', 'Routes to the observed OCI provisioning failures. Does not interpret zero-range metadata as a valid zero-memory configuration or invent a quota cause. Distinguishes A2 core/OCPU accounting from requested OCPUs and notes that 8GiB, 6GiB and omitted-memory attempts all failed. Checks image compatibility, shape metadata, limits and actual allocation independently. Explains positive limits do not establish physical capacity and does not claim cluster acceptance when no VM launched.', '[]', '{"id": 16, "name": "oci-zero-memory-ratio-and-capacity", "prompt": "OCI refuses my AutoMQ A2 launch with Invalid ratio of memory in GB to OCPUs. Current ratio: 4.0. Valid ratio range: 0 - 0. I requested 1 OCPU and 8GB, the shape says flexible and my limits are positive. A1 in the same region says Out of host capacity. Should I set memory to zero or assume the provider doubled my OCPUs?", "expected_behaviour": "Routes to the observed OCI provisioning failures. Does not interpret zero-range metadata as a valid zero-memory configuration or invent a quota cause. Distinguishes A2 core/OCPU accounting from requested OCPUs and notes that 8GiB, 6GiB and omitted-memory attempts all failed. Checks image compatibility, shape metadata, limits and actual allocation independently. Explains positive limits do not establish physical capacity and does not claim cluster acceptance when no VM launched."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 17, '17', 'oci-service-user-primary-email', 'My AutoMQ OCI storage stage created buckets, then oci_identity_user.application failed with IdcsConversionError: The primary email must be specified. The provider let me omit email. Should I delete the state and rerun?', 'Identifies the observed Identity Domains requirement. Sets explicit tenancy-unique automq-oci-user-email, preserves existing stage state, and distinguishes a failed user operation from rollback of buckets and other resources. Does not claim successful broker acceptance from a completed storage stage.', '[]', '{"id": 17, "name": "oci-service-user-primary-email", "prompt": "My AutoMQ OCI storage stage created buckets, then oci_identity_user.application failed with IdcsConversionError: The primary email must be specified. The provider let me omit email. Should I delete the state and rerun?", "expected_behaviour": "Identifies the observed Identity Domains requirement. Sets explicit tenancy-unique automq-oci-user-email, preserves existing stage state, and distinguishes a failed user operation from rollback of buckets and other resources. Does not claim successful broker acceptance from a completed storage stage."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 18, '18', 'oci-failed-create-needs-absence-proof', 'OCI rejected all AutoMQ VM launches. I changed A2 to A1, but compute still refuses the failed create. The node state has serial1 and no params. Can I clear the failed operation in the journal?', 'Uses the pinned compute library explicit OCI recovery function instead of manual journal edits. Requires current failed operation IDs, empty valid state and complete native VM/boot-volume absence checks under the journal lease, across the original compartment and domains. Treats terminating resources and empty CLI stdout as insufficient absence evidence. Notes resource-name matching limits after out-of-band renames.', '[]', '{"id": 18, "name": "oci-failed-create-needs-absence-proof", "prompt": "OCI rejected all AutoMQ VM launches. I changed A2 to A1, but compute still refuses the failed create. The node state has serial1 and no params. Can I clear the failed operation in the journal?", "expected_behaviour": "Uses the pinned compute library explicit OCI recovery function instead of manual journal edits. Requires current failed operation IDs, empty valid state and complete native VM/boot-volume absence checks under the journal lease, across the original compartment and domains. Treats terminating resources and empty CLI stdout as insufficient absence evidence. Notes resource-name matching limits after out-of-band renames."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 19, '19', 'oci-partial-delete-skips-owned-resources', 'No AutoMQ VM ever launched on OCI, but I have three owned buckets, an NSG and application IAM keys. After recovering the failed creates, delete jumps straight to backend finalization, which refuses active state. Does no running VM mean I can bypass that check and delete the backend?', 'Identifies the partial-deployment deletion routing defect. Distinguishes inspection error from verified absence, keeps finalizer refusal intact, and requires owned application storage/IAM, shared resources and SSH key cleanup before state finalization. Explains that declared never-created nodes with validated empty state need a journal/state transition rather than provider destruction. Does not infer a successful cleanup from the absence of VMs or a safe refusal.', '[]', '{"id": 19, "name": "oci-partial-delete-skips-owned-resources", "prompt": "No AutoMQ VM ever launched on OCI, but I have three owned buckets, an NSG and application IAM keys. After recovering the failed creates, delete jumps straight to backend finalization, which refuses active state. Does no running VM mean I can bypass that check and delete the backend?", "expected_behaviour": "Identifies the partial-deployment deletion routing defect. Distinguishes inspection error from verified absence, keeps finalizer refusal intact, and requires owned application storage/IAM, shared resources and SSH key cleanup before state finalization. Explains that declared never-created nodes with validated empty state need a journal/state transition rather than provider destruction. Does not infer a successful cleanup from the absence of VMs or a safe refusal."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 20, '20', 'oci-bucket-update-wrong-method', 'My AutoMQ state bucket marker says deleting and the journal is retired, but native PUT on the bucket endpoint gets 404 NotFound during finalization. No versions have been purged yet. Should I assume the bucket vanished and remove its local state?', 'Identifies the verified UpdateBucket method mismatch: native OCI bucket updates use POST. Checks operation and method before interpreting 404 as absence. Preserves deleting marker and retired journal, resumes normal finalization after the method correction, and does not create a replacement backend or bypass ownership guards.', '[]', '{"id": 20, "name": "oci-bucket-update-wrong-method", "prompt": "My AutoMQ state bucket marker says deleting and the journal is retired, but native PUT on the bucket endpoint gets 404 NotFound during finalization. No versions have been purged yet. Should I assume the bucket vanished and remove its local state?", "expected_behaviour": "Identifies the verified UpdateBucket method mismatch: native OCI bucket updates use POST. Checks operation and method before interpreting 404 as absence. Preserves deleting marker and retired journal, resumes normal finalization after the method correction, and does not create a replacement backend or bypass ownership guards."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 21, '21', 'oci-retired-held-journal-resume', 'The AutoMQ compute journal is retired, its identity is right, and backend finalization was interrupted while holding its lease. Normal delete now says compute state unavailable; legacy monolithic state requires explicit migration. Is this actually old state that I need to migrate?', 'Identifies the verified classification-order defect. Requires valid journal schema and matching deployment identity before recognizing retired status, then routes to guarded backend finalization even if its old finalizer lease is held. Keeps active held journals excluded, and does not infer legacy state from the generic error or manually clear ownership evidence.', '[]', '{"id": 21, "name": "oci-retired-held-journal-resume", "prompt": "The AutoMQ compute journal is retired, its identity is right, and backend finalization was interrupted while holding its lease. Normal delete now says compute state unavailable; legacy monolithic state requires explicit migration. Is this actually old state that I need to migrate?", "expected_behaviour": "Identifies the verified classification-order defect. Requires valid journal schema and matching deployment identity before recognizing retired status, then routes to guarded backend finalization even if its old finalizer lease is held. Keeps active held journals excluded, and does not infer legacy state from the generic error or manually clear ownership evidence."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 22, '22', 'oci-get-readiness-after-list-success', 'Our new OCI AutoMQ account passes ListObjects and the ops conditional-write gate, then adopt gets SignatureDoesNotMatch reading a colors-ready marker. Does that mean the marker is missing or that I should generate a new credential?', 'Identifies operation-specific readiness observed on September 12. Distinguishes authenticated NoSuchKey from a signature failure, requires positive exact-byte GET and authenticated missing-object probes in both application buckets, preserves existing generated credentials and secrets, and retries a bounded complete readiness check. Does not confuse VM allocation or an ops-only precondition pass with broker acceptance.', '[]', '{"id": 22, "name": "oci-get-readiness-after-list-success", "prompt": "Our new OCI AutoMQ account passes ListObjects and the ops conditional-write gate, then adopt gets SignatureDoesNotMatch reading a colors-ready marker. Does that mean the marker is missing or that I should generate a new credential?", "expected_behaviour": "Identifies operation-specific readiness observed on September 12. Distinguishes authenticated NoSuchKey from a signature failure, requires positive exact-byte GET and authenticated missing-object probes in both application buckets, preserves existing generated credentials and secrets, and retries a bounded complete readiness check. Does not confuse VM allocation or an ops-only precondition pass with broker acceptance."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 23, '23', 'oci-inactive-ufw-raw-reject', 'All three OCI A1 hosts say UFW inactive. SSH and ping work, but iptables-save shows a final INPUT REJECT after the SSH allow rule. Should I flush iptables before starting AutoMQ?', 'Recognizes the observed OCI raw host filter despite inactive UFW. Requires scoped Kafka client/private controller/inter-broker accepts before the terminal rejection, preserves OCI InstanceServices link-local rules, and verifies persistence and real TCP reachability. Does not flush provider rules or infer cluster acceptance from firewall configuration.', '[]', '{"id": 23, "name": "oci-inactive-ufw-raw-reject", "prompt": "All three OCI A1 hosts say UFW inactive. SSH and ping work, but iptables-save shows a final INPUT REJECT after the SSH allow rule. Should I flush iptables before starting AutoMQ?", "expected_behaviour": "Recognizes the observed OCI raw host filter despite inactive UFW. Requires scoped Kafka client/private controller/inter-broker accepts before the terminal rejection, preserves OCI InstanceServices link-local rules, and verifies persistence and real TCP reachability. Does not flush provider rules or infer cluster acceptance from firewall configuration."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 24, '24', 'oci-probe-leftovers-block-adoption', 'AutoMQ adoption refuses a nonempty OCI ops bucket after readiness failed twice. The only objects appear to be test keys under preconditions. Can I empty the bucket and retry?', 'Routes to the observed readiness cleanup failure. Requires exact key and synthetic-body verification before removing only recorded probe objects, preserves ownership/genesis markers and unrelated data, and uses a persistent exact-key ledger so failed cleanup retries cannot leave untracked probes.', '[]', '{"id": 24, "name": "oci-probe-leftovers-block-adoption", "prompt": "AutoMQ adoption refuses a nonempty OCI ops bucket after readiness failed twice. The only objects appear to be test keys under preconditions. Can I empty the bucket and retry?", "expected_behaviour": "Routes to the observed readiness cleanup failure. Requires exact key and synthetic-body verification before removing only recorded probe objects, preserves ownership/genesis markers and unrelated data, and uses a persistent exact-key ledger so failed cleanup retries cannot leave untracked probes."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 25, '25', 'oci-firewall-platform-lost-after-reboot', 'After reboot our OCI AutoMQ firewall chain returned and the broker is healthy, but INPUT/FORWARD rejections and InstanceServices OUTPUT rules disappeared. Apt logs say installing UFW removed iptables-persistent and netfilter-persistent. Did the firewall test pass?', 'Recognizes the verified package conflict and distinguishes application health from firewall persistence. Preserves/restores native platform persistence, avoids installing conflicting UFW on the native path, compares both owned and platform rules after a real reboot, and keeps the failed audit historical until a corrected audit passes.', '[]', '{"id": 25, "name": "oci-firewall-platform-lost-after-reboot", "prompt": "After reboot our OCI AutoMQ firewall chain returned and the broker is healthy, but INPUT/FORWARD rejections and InstanceServices OUTPUT rules disappeared. Apt logs say installing UFW removed iptables-persistent and netfilter-persistent. Did the firewall test pass?", "expected_behaviour": "Recognizes the verified package conflict and distinguishes application health from firewall persistence. Preserves/restores native platform persistence, avoids installing conflicting UFW on the native path, compares both owned and platform rules after a real reboot, and keeps the failed audit historical until a corrected audit passes."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.eval_case (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, source_id, name, prompt, expected_output, assertions, raw_case) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'evals/evals.json', 26, '26', 'literal-ip-tls-and-graceful-stop-gates', 'Our AutoMQ acceptance skips certificate checks because getent cannot reverse-resolve broker IPs. It also reports zero-second failover, timed after docker stop returns. Can I report TLS and abrupt-crash recovery as passing?', 'Requires direct CA and IP-SAN verification for literal IPs without a PTR prerequisite. Explains that a skipped certificate check is not a pass and post-graceful-stop timing is not abrupt-crash evidence. Requires pre-fault timing, abrupt stop, live leadership targeting and stopped-victim guards before reporting crash recovery.', '[]', '{"id": 26, "name": "literal-ip-tls-and-graceful-stop-gates", "prompt": "Our AutoMQ acceptance skips certificate checks because getent cannot reverse-resolve broker IPs. It also reports zero-second failover, timed after docker stop returns. Can I report TLS and abrupt-crash recovery as passing?", "expected_behaviour": "Requires direct CA and IP-SAN verification for literal IPs without a PTR prerequisite. Explains that a skipped certificate check is not a pass and post-graceful-stop timing is not abrupt-crash evidence. Requires pre-fault timing, abrupt stop, live leadership targeting and stopped-victim guards before reporting crash recovery."}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.source_file (skill_slug, version_id, path, content, sha256, line_count, git_mode) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', decode('', 'base64'), '8d46b6b9922d134e18e478fa483cb9b818e92551a239cef5736c3277610dc531', 135, '100644') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 0, 'acceptance', 'Acceptance doctrine', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine']::text[], 1, 9, '# Acceptance doctrine

What this stack proves, how, and — more usefully — which reassuring results
mean nothing.

The gates live in the companion package (`github.com/getcolors/automq`) as
`smoke.sh` (on the hosts) and `acceptance.sh` (from the workstation). This file
is the reasoning; it carries no copies.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 1, 'acceptance', 'The ordering rule', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','The ordering rule']::text[], 10, 15, '## The ordering rule

On-host gates run **before** the storage readiness marker is written. The
marker means "this cluster was proved", and a later converge trusts it. Writing
it when the containers start would make it mean "something was launched".

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 2, 'acceptance', 'What each gate is defending against', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','What each gate is defending against']::text[], 16, 17, '## What each gate is defending against

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 3, 'acceptance', 'On the hosts', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','What each gate is defending against','On the hosts']::text[], 18, 29, '### On the hosts

| Gate | Defends against |
|---|---|
| Three brokers in cluster metadata | Processes that started but never registered |
| `kafka-metadata-quorum describe --status`: 3 voters **and a leader** | A quorum that lists voters while electing nobody. Static voters stay listed when dead — the leader is the part that carries information |
| N records produced and consumed back, exact match | A broker that accepts writes it cannot serve |
| Objects present in the **data** bucket | The whole architecture. Without this the cluster could be writing to local disk and every other gate still passes |
| Objects present in the **ops** bucket | Ops path silently disabled |
| Wrong password refused; unauthenticated connection refused | An endpoint that accepts everything passes every other gate in the file |
| Client principal denied a cluster-level operation, and denied a topic outside its prefix | Authentication mistaken for authorization |

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 4, 'acceptance', 'From the workstation', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','What each gate is defending against','From the workstation']::text[], 30, 42, '### From the workstation

| Gate | Defends against |
|---|---|
| Each public name resolves and completes a **verified** TLS handshake | A certificate that exists on disk but is not what the port serves |
| `kcat` over SASL_SSL lists all brokers and round-trips records | Something that works only from inside the VPC |
| Wrong password refused *at the public endpoint* | An internal-only auth boundary |
| Client principal cannot write outside its prefix | ACLs that exist but are not enforced on the path clients use |
| **Targeted** failover (below) | The failover gate that proves nothing |
| Consumer-group offsets survive the outage | RF=1 `__consumer_offsets` losing a group''s position |
| A restarted controller re-authenticates and the quorum recovers | A controller mechanism that only works at genesis |
| p99 produce latency, reported not asserted | Pretending a cross-provider storage tier is free |

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 5, 'acceptance', 'The two gates that are easy to fake', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','The two gates that are easy to fake']::text[], 43, 62, '## The two gates that are easy to fake

**Failover.** Producing unkeyed records to a six-partition topic and killing a
broker can pass without ever touching the broker that died. The gate must work
from the leadership map: read which node leads which partition, pick the victim
from that, produce keyed records to exactly that partition, then kill it.

Do not fix the victim to a particular node index either. Leadership drifts
after an earlier failover, so "kill node 2" fails with *no partition is led by
node 2* on a completely healthy cluster — a gate failing because of its own
assumption. Recreate the topic each run and derive the victim.

Likewise "it rejoined" must be measured, not observed: the broker re-registers,
its replication lag is bounded, and its log-end offset has caught up to the
leader''s. A static voter remains listed in the voter set the entire time it is
dead, so the voter list alone says nothing.

**Storage.** "The cluster works" is not evidence the storage tier is object
storage. Only listing objects in the bucket is.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 6, 'acceptance', 'What RF=1 does and does not buy', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','What RF=1 does and does not buy']::text[], 63, 74, '## What RF=1 does and does not buy

Durability comes from the object store: a produce is acknowledged once the
record is there, so losing a broker loses no bytes, and that *is* provable —
pre-failure records are still readable after the leader dies.

Availability is a different claim. A partition whose leader dies is unwritable
until it is reassigned, and `__consumer_offsets` is RF=1 like everything else,
so a group''s committed position lives on one broker too. Both windows are
measured and reported rather than asserted away. Anyone reading RF=1 as a
misconfiguration should be pointed at this paragraph.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 7, 'acceptance', 'Idempotency is part of acceptance', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','Idempotency is part of acceptance']::text[], 75, 85, '## Idempotency is part of acceptance

Gates run on every converge, against a cluster that keeps its data. A gate
that counts absolute totals passes the first time and fails forever after —
against a perfectly healthy cluster. Recreate the gate''s own topic (waiting out
Kafka''s asynchronous deletion before reusing the name), or tag every record and
consumer group with a per-run value so each gate counts what this run produced.

The first converge is the one you watch, which is exactly why this defect
survives review.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 8, 'acceptance', 'What this doctrine does not cover', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','What this doctrine does not cover']::text[], 86, 98, '## What this doctrine does not cover

Stated so nothing here reads as a stronger claim than the build supports:

- The consumer-group gate proves committed offsets survive the outage. It does
  **not** compute which `__consumer_offsets` partition holds the group and force
  the victim to be that partition''s leader, so the RF=1 coordinator-loss case
  is exercised only when placement happens to line up.
- The certificate rollout serializes restarts by mutual exclusion (a lease),
  not by a fixed node order with per-generation acknowledgements. One at a time
  is the safety property; ordering is not.
- Transactional workloads are untested; see `pins.md`.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 9, 'acceptance', 'Two failure modes that mimic success', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','Two failure modes that mimic success']::text[], 99, 106, '## Two failure modes that mimic success

- **Exit codes.** Every command in a converge can exit zero while the cluster
  is unusable. Ask the system what it has.
- **A gate that cannot fail.** If a gate would pass on a broken cluster, it is
  decoration. The clearest example here: a generic produce/consume round trip
  after killing a broker.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 10, 'acceptance', 'OCI assessment scope, 2026-09-11', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','OCI assessment scope, 2026-09-11']::text[], 107, 120, '## OCI assessment scope, 2026-09-11

The OCI assessment did not launch a broker. Shape validation and physical
capacity errors prevented VM allocation. It therefore did not run the
on-host or public Kafka gates above, continuity, targeted failover, or repeated
full cluster convergence.

The actual managed storage stage passed synthetic object round trips,
conditional writes, lease exclusion and state-bucket denial with its scoped
application identity. A second successful storage create had a no-change
plan. These are separate storage claims, recorded in `oci.md`; synthetic
objects are not evidence of AutoMQ object creation or acknowledged records.


') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/acceptance.md', 11, 'acceptance', 'OCI A1 assessment scope, 2026-09-12', ARRAY['Acceptance doctrine','OCI A1 assessment scope, 2026-09-12']::text[], 121, 135, '## OCI A1 assessment scope, 2026-09-12

A different OCI account passed three complete converges with nine host and 16
public gates. The first two used source `5aac5a3`; the third used `6b76c01`
after a firewall idempotency correction. Targeted abrupt-stop recovery took
11, 11 and 10 seconds, with all 100 acknowledged records preserved each time.
Timers began before the fault, and the gate checked that the victim remained
stopped. These are abrupt broker-stop observations, not disk-loss or AZ-loss
tests. The consumer-offset placement limitation above still applies.

The independent continuity topic survived reboots and repeated convergence.
A repaired real reboot preserved owned and platform firewall rules. The
cluster remains running with destruction protection; September 11 cleanup
evidence concerns a different account''s partial deployment. See
`oci-2026-09-12.md` for exact pins, evidence and scope.
') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.source_file (skill_slug, version_id, path, content, sha256, line_count, git_mode) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/aws.md', decode('', 'base64'), '8362d4cd54961cfe91c52c7e63f1226b55bae8ea10b5b984f4602b5e498b52ad', 64, '100644') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/aws.md', 0, 'prose', 'AWS lifecycle and verification scope', ARRAY['AWS lifecycle and verification scope']::text[], 1, 7, '# AWS lifecycle and verification scope

The live assessment on 2026-09-10 used profile `automq-aws`, three Ubuntu 24.04
`t3.large` nodes in `us-east-1a`, and the AutoMQ 1.7.4 digest in `pins.md`.
Its evidence is [the deployment verification report](https://github.com/getcolors/automq-aws/blob/main/verification.md).
All AWS resources were deleted after the assessment.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/aws.md', 1, 'prose', 'Buckets have a deployment lifecycle', ARRAY['AWS lifecycle and verification scope','Buckets have a deployment lifecycle']::text[], 8, 26, '## Buckets have a deployment lifecycle

The deployment created a state bucket, separate data and ops buckets, and an
application IAM identity restricted to those two application buckets. The
actual application credentials could list both app buckets but received HTTP
403 on the state bucket''s `HeadBucket` request.

Deletion stopped all three brokers before removing application storage and its
IAM identity. Compute and the owned SSH keypair followed. Backend finalization
removed the state bucket and its versioned objects last. A repeated delete
passed with no backend and without SSH access. The default destruction guard
refused deletion before destructive stages.

These were owned resources, not external buckets adopted by the original R2
configuration. Bucket ownership must determine cleanup behavior. The final
provider audit found no owned VPC resources, machines, root volumes, keypair,
application IAM identity or buckets. Local owned SSH key files and aliases were
also absent.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/aws.md', 2, 'prose', 'IP certificates require explicit client trust', ARRAY['AWS lifecycle and verification scope','IP certificates require explicit client trust']::text[], 27, 37, '## IP certificates require explicit client trust

This assessment used a deployment CA and public IP SANs, with no DNS provider.
Every external TLS gate verified both the exported CA and the IP identity.
Clients passed the exported CA to kcat through `ssl.ca.location`. The CA private
key remained on the issuer node.

This configuration does not establish the Cloudflare DNS-01 renewal claim.
The original single-issuer and serialized-restart requirements still apply to
certificate distribution on combined broker/controller nodes.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/aws.md', 3, 'prose', 'CLI memory can kill an otherwise healthy broker', ARRAY['AWS lifecycle and verification scope','CLI memory can kill an otherwise healthy broker']::text[], 38, 46, '## CLI memory can kill an otherwise healthy broker

Kafka CLI and healthcheck processes inherited the broker''s 2 GiB minimum heap.
The live run observed an OOM kill. The package fixed those processes at a
256 MiB maximum heap and updated existing brokers one at a time with readiness
checks. Both full successful runs completed without another observed OOM kill.
Check the CLI environment as well as the broker heap when a gate coincides with
an OOM event.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/aws.md', 4, 'prose', 'What the live gates proved', ARRAY['AWS lifecycle and verification scope','What the live gates proved']::text[], 47, 64, '## What the live gates proved

Two complete converges passed, the second with immutable published dependencies
and all development overrides unset. On-host checks verified a 500-record exact
match. External checks passed all 16 gates, including a 200-record public round
trip, access refusals, a targeted outage, and a controller restart. A separate
50-record topic remained an exact match across the second converge.

Node 2 led the tested partition. All 100 acknowledged pre-outage records
survived its controlled container stop. The partition accepted writes one second
after `docker stop` returned. The restarted broker registered with zero lag and
matching log end offsets. Checked consumer-group offsets survived.

The one-second observation starts after the stop command returns. It is not a
crash-recovery guarantee. The test did not force the consumer coordinator onto
the victim, destroy a disk, lose an AZ, or test transactions. A short 20,000-record
burst reported 1,530.57 records/s and 11,042 ms p99. It is not a steady-state
capacity benchmark.
') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.source_file (skill_slug, version_id, path, content, sha256, line_count, git_mode) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/contract.md', decode('', 'base64'), '542a9468dda07f2edabfee975ebc274afcd0f9f06fd87ee35f391eff3f9b25a5', 113, '100644') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/contract.md', 0, 'contract', 'The configuration contract, as the source defines it', ARRAY['The configuration contract, as the source defines it']::text[], 1, 9, '# The configuration contract, as the source defines it

What the broker actually requires for a three-node, fully authenticated,
S3-backed cluster — established by reading AutoMQ 1.7.4 (Kafka 3.9.1) and by
converging it, not from a quick start.

Settings are named here so you can check yours. The rendered file is the
companion package''s (`github.com/getcolors/automq`); this is not a copy of it.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/contract.md', 1, 'contract', 'Listener layout', ARRAY['The configuration contract, as the source defines it','Listener layout']::text[], 10, 34, '## Listener layout

Three listeners, and the split is a security boundary rather than a
convenience:

| Listener | Protocol | Bound to | Principal |
|---|---|---|---|
| `CONTROLLER` | `SASL_PLAINTEXT` + `PLAIN` | private address | a controller principal |
| `INTERNAL` | `SASL_PLAINTEXT` + `SCRAM-SHA-512` | private address | an inter-broker principal |
| `EXTERNAL` | `SASL_SSL` + `SCRAM-SHA-512` | `0.0.0.0` | client and admin principals |

Non-obvious requirements, each of which cost a converge:

- **`controller.listener.names=CONTROLLER` is mandatory** and is *not* implied
  by `listener.security.protocol.map`. Without it the broker does not start.
- **`CONTROLLER` must not appear in `advertised.listeners`.** Kafka rejects it.
- **Every SASL listener needs its own JAAS entry**, including the public SCRAM
  one — which carries no username or password, because a SCRAM server
  validates against the metadata log. Omitting it is failure-catalogue 5.
- `listener.name.controller.sasl.enabled.mechanisms` must be set explicitly;
  `sasl.mechanism.controller.protocol` alone does not imply it.
- Binding a listener to a **specific private address** means the container
  cannot use bridge networking — it cannot bind an address that belongs only to
  the host. Host networking is a requirement, not a preference.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/contract.md', 2, 'contract', 'Why the controller listener uses PLAIN', ARRAY['The configuration contract, as the source defines it','Why the controller listener uses PLAIN']::text[], 35, 55, '## Why the controller listener uses PLAIN

SCRAM credentials live in the metadata log that the controller quorum must
form in order to serve. Using SCRAM there makes quorum formation depend on
something only a formed quorum can provide.

Genesis `--add-scram` records are the documented way out of that cycle, so
SCRAM on the controller listener is *arguable* — but it is not a safe
load-bearing assumption on this version (KAFKA-15513 reports exactly this
failure, and Kafka''s own test harness still marks controller-quorum SCRAM
unsupported).

**PLAIN from a static JAAS file has no bootstrap dependency at all, which
makes it correct whether or not controller SCRAM works.** A design that is
right under both outcomes beats one that is right under a contested one. The
transport stays on the private network.

PLAIN carries **both directions in one entry**, because it has no credential
store behind it: `username`/`password` is who this node connects *as*, and
`user_<name>=` is who it accepts.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/contract.md', 3, 'contract', 'SCRAM credentials at genesis', ARRAY['The configuration contract, as the source defines it','SCRAM credentials at genesis']::text[], 56, 79, '## SCRAM credentials at genesis

`--add-scram` with a **plaintext** password derives a *random salt per
invocation*. Formatting three voters that way writes three divergent bootstrap
records for one user.

Compute each principal''s salt and salted password **once** and pass the
explicit form to every genesis format:

```
SCRAM-SHA-512=[name=…,salt=<b64>,saltedpassword=<b64>,iterations=<n>]
```

`saltedpassword` is `base64(PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512(password, salt, iterations))`
with the digest''s natural output length. This was verified byte-identical to
Kafka''s own derivation; see failure-catalogue 3.

Replacement nodes are formatted **without** bootstrap records — KRaft
replicates SCRAM credentials in the metadata log and a replacement voter
catches up from the quorum.

**Drift can only be detected by authenticating.** `kafka-configs --describe`
exposes iterations and salt, never anything comparable to a plaintext.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/contract.md', 4, 'contract', 'Authorization', ARRAY['The configuration contract, as the source defines it','Authorization']::text[], 80, 90, '## Authorization

`StandardAuthorizer` with `allow.everyone.if.no.acl.found=false`. The public
client principal is **not** a superuser: prefixed `Describe/Read/Write` on
topics and `Describe/Read` on groups, and nothing else — no `Create`, no
`Alter`, no `ClusterAction`, no `TransactionalId`.

Making `ANONYMOUS` a superuser to simplify the private listeners turns both of
them into unauthenticated cluster-root endpoints. Network isolation reduces
that exposure; it does not remove it.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/contract.md', 5, 'contract', 'Storage', ARRAY['The configuration contract, as the source defines it','Storage']::text[], 91, 108, '## Storage

```
s3.data.buckets=0@s3://<data>?region=…&endpoint=…&pathStyle=true
s3.ops.buckets=1@s3://<ops>?region=…&endpoint=…&pathStyle=true
s3.wal.path=0@s3://<data>?…&batchInterval=<ms>&maxBytesInBatch=<bytes>
```

- Data and ops are addressed by **distinct bucket ids**; the WAL points at the
  **data** bucket.
- `pathStyle=true` for an account-level S3-compatible endpoint.
- `region=auto` works against Cloudflare R2.
- **`batchInterval` is the lever** when the object store is a different
  provider or region from the compute: every acknowledgement waits on an S3
  write, so it is latency traded for throughput.
- A bucket belongs to one cluster. See `pins.md` for why no prefix can confine
  AutoMQ inside a shared one.

') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.section (skill_slug, version_id, path, ordinal, kind, heading, heading_path, start_line, end_line, body) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/contract.md', 6, 'contract', 'Replication factor', ARRAY['The configuration contract, as the source defines it','Replication factor']::text[], 109, 113, '## Replication factor

RF=1 for internal topics and as the default, which is upstream''s own shipped
configuration. Durability is the object store. See `acceptance.md` for what
that does and does not buy.
') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO catalog.source_file (skill_slug, version_id, path, content, sha256, line_count, git_mode) VALUES ('automq-multi-node', 'c672ce61d4cb64c46e8c1f14148a9def17605719ee1b665e0e2d96ee24f85808', 'references/failure-catalogue.md', decode('', 'base64'), 'b3a94917f4cdb0496ee1454286ccb242638a0fbf331275e5b57fb6970e5b9788', 461, '100644') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
